# review-delivery

`wait-mate:` means the Crew stopped. It may be done, or it may be saying "I could not, here is why".
Never relay it to the captain as a result until you have checked it.
Start from the item `{{.MatevBin}} wake {{.ProjectName}}` printed: the branch summary, the hand-back or report path, its `## For the next crew` section, its acceptance table, its `## Deviations from Build` and `## Still open`, and the reviews recorded for it. Peek nothing, and do not cat the hand-back: what step 2 checks is in the wake.

## A ship

1. Rebuild the contract from `{{.CrewsDir}}/<id>/brief.md` (`## Captain's words` with anything appended, then `## Build` and `## Acceptance`), never from the Crew's account of what it was asked.
2. Read `{{.CrewsDir}}/<id>/handback.md` against it: every `## Acceptance` line has a row; each row says `pass` with a command and output that show it; `## Deviations from Build` holds nothing the captain's words did not ask for; `## Still open` holds nothing that was the task's to settle.
   A missing hand-back is a one-line send asking for it, not a reason to read the diff instead.
   A failed step 2 is settled before anyone reviews, whatever the review setting: a deviation from the captain's words, a part of `## Build` or of a spec it names that was dropped, or a choice `## Open decisions` or the captain owned, is a correction (see Corrections) or, when the Crew's choice would widen the contract, the captain's question. A reviewer never decides a deviation; it costs a review round to learn what the hand-back already says.
3. Whether an independent reviewer looks at it is the project's setting, which `wake` prints as `reviews:` and `{{.MatevBin}} project review {{.ProjectName}}` prints in full. `off`: skip to Landing once step 2 holds. Undecided: ask the captain first, as rule 3 says, and record their answer before you go on. `on`: once step 2 holds, start an independent reviewer and end your turn: `{{.MatevBin}} review {{.ProjectName}} <id> --id <review-id>`.
   With a tracker the review id is the issue and the round, `<issue>-r1`, then `-r2` for `--since` (`hvn-57-r1` reviews `hvn-57-trip-repo` and `hvn-57-f1` alike); without one, `<id>-r1`, shortening `<id>` to fit 24 characters.
   Without `--model` and `--effort` it picks the table's review profile by the size of the change; pass `--harness` alone to choose the reviewer's harness, and a full profile only when the change carries the blast radius `new-task` sizes as large.
4. When the reviewer hands back, read `{{.MatevBin}} report {{.ProjectName}} <review-id> --summary`, then `{{.MatevBin}} review {{.ProjectName}} <id> --check <review-id>`.
   A failed check is never approval. Send demonstrated defects for correction; for incomplete verification, resolve the evidence gap and request a new review. Missing credentials or a skipped live test call for evidence, not an invented code fix. A stale review needs a fresh review of the current revision.

A summary, a diff stat or your own impression is not approval. Read the full patch yourself (`mate diff {{.ProjectName}} <id> --full`) only when the captain asks you to review directly.
Read-only git in the Crew's worktree is allowed for what `mate diff` does not print; anything that writes is not, and landing is `mate land` alone.

## Corrections

A correction inside the contract is yours to send, in one line: a `fail` row, a criterion with no row, a deviation `## Build` did not allow, a diff that does not do what the captain's words ask.
One that would widen the contract goes to the captain first, as `wake` says, and the Crew waits.
A Crew that has handed back gets your line in a fresh session (`mate send` opens it) that starts from its brief, its hand-back, your line and the diff stat since the last review. Put what it must not repeat after the words `Already tried`.
When you tell a Crew to wait for something (a check, a build, another branch), tell it to wait once, with one blocking command and a long timeout. Never a sleep loop, never "check again every few minutes".

The fix after a review is reviewed from the commit that review saw, never from scratch:

- A fix that touches code, or only docs: `{{.MatevBin}} review {{.ProjectName}} <id> --id <new-review-id> --since <review-id>`. The reviewer gets the diff since that commit and the earlier findings as a checklist, and reruns a gate only when the diff touches it.
- After a rebase or a merge of the default branch, compare the ranges yourself, read-only: `git range-diff <base>..<reviewed-sha> <base>..HEAD` in the worktree. When `--since` refuses because the branch was rewritten and the ranges show no semantic change, start a full review and tell the reviewer, in one `mate send` line with `--queue`, that you compared the ranges at `<reviewed-sha>` and it should confirm and rerun nothing.
- Two rounds of review at most. A third correction on the same theme is the captain's call: report the evidence and your recommendation, and the Crew waits.

## Landing

Once the review passes, or with review `off` once the hand-back checks out, land the work with one command:

```text
{{.MatevBin}} land {{.ProjectName}} <id> [--review <review-id>]
```

It is `mate merge` and every record that follows, in order, each on one line: the merge (with `--review` pinned to the commit the reviewer saw; `gh pr merge` for a pull request ship; `crew stop` for a pull request the watcher already saw merged), the crew closed with its teardown, the learning record's head written from the hand-back's `Learning:` line, the backlog entry to `## Done`, the tracker through the captain's `tracker.land` command, and a `next:` line with the open crews, the lanes, the queue and the next-work setting. Read that output instead of running those commands yourself.
Every refusal is `mate merge`'s and happens before anything is touched: `merge refused: needs-rebase` is one line to the Crew, "rebase onto <default branch> and hand back with wait-mate", and land after its next hand-back; the rest are in `mate-commands`.
A line after the merge that says `not updated`, `not moved` or `not written` is a record it could not make: the merge stands, so do that one thing yourself (`tracker: ... mark it done yourself` is the usual one, when the captain gave no `tracker.land` command).
A `left behind:` part names what the teardown could not remove and why: tell the captain in one line, it does not hold the task.
Report what it printed, in outcome words, then keep the work moving, as AGENTS.md says.

`task-learning` is the one judgment it leaves to you. `land` writes the record's head from the hand-back only when you wrote none (`Learning: pending`), so a hand-back whose evidence shows a real finding gets your record first, as `task-learning` says, or your one line as `--learning "<text>"`; a `predictions: ... still open` line names the brief's predictions you still have to close.
When memory `## Captain` says the captain merges this kind of work, report it as ready instead, naming the branch or the pull request.
New reviews pass only with Findings and Verification gaps both set to none; Notes never block. For a legacy review that passed but left findings standing, record them first: add a line under `## Queued` with `mate backlog add`, or name the tracker issue, and land again with `--followups backlog:<text>` or `--followups <TRACKER-id>`.
A passing review's `## Notes` open no correction round: land, and record a note worth keeping with `mate backlog add` or in the tracker, as you judge; it needs no `--followups`.

A pull request ship (`--deliver pr`) hands back with `pr-open:`; review its branch exactly as above. It fixes corrections on the same branch and pushes; the same pull request follows. `land` runs `gh pr merge` for it.
A watcher follows every open pull request and wakes you with `pr-merged:` or `pr-closed:`. When a Crew opened one without it and no console is open, start it: `{{.MatevBin}} pr watch {{.ProjectName}} <id> <url>`.

- **`pr-merged:`** - `wake` lists the Crew as a `landed` item with its `learning:` line until it is closed. Run `{{.MatevBin}} land {{.ProjectName}} <id>`: it takes the merged pull request as landed, even after a squash or rebase merge, closes the Crew and makes the same records. If the line says the checkout was `skipped:`, tell the captain their copy of the default branch is behind. Then keep the work moving.
- **`pr-merged: the app landed <id>`** - in auto mode the watcher ran `mate land` for you. Do only what it says is `left for you` (a tracker it could not update, an open prediction), then keep the work moving. While other Crews are working it does not wake you: the same news comes as an `app did: landed <id>` item in your next digest.
- **`pr-closed:`** - it was closed unmerged. Find out why (its comments, through a scout if they are long), then either send the Crew the rework, telling it to reopen the pull request (`gh pr reopen`) and run `pr watch` again, or tell the captain it was turned down.

## A scout

Read `{{.MatevBin}} report {{.ProjectName}} <id> --summary`, then the parts you need to verify its claims; the report is the whole deliverable.
A scout is done when its report answers every question under `## Deliverable`. Then:

1. Add to `{{.ProjectDoc}}` what its `## Durable facts` says that the file does not, with the report as source, as `stow` says.
   When they name a command that removes what a repo starts per worktree (a `make down`, a `worktree-destroy`), record it so every crew stop runs it: `{{.MatevBin}} project repo teardown {{.ProjectName}} <repo> "<command>"`, and say so to the captain in one line.
2. Relay the findings to the captain as findings, not "it's done".
3. Run `task-learning`, then close it with `{{.MatevBin}} crew stop {{.ProjectName}} <id>`. Keep it open only while you still need a follow-up answer from the same Crew.

## Closing a task

A Crew's task ends when you or the captain decide, never when the Crew says so; until `mate land` or `crew stop` runs, it stays in flight.
A ship is closed when its work has landed: `mate land`, which also finds a pull request the captain merged landed.
Unlanded work is thrown away with `crew stop --discard` only on the captain's explicit word, and that records the Crew `failed`.
Before `crew stop` closes a task (scout, failed or abandoned), run `task-learning` if this evidence revision has no result yet, then `{{.MatevBin}} backlog done {{.ProjectName}} <id>`, and update the captain's tracker when the task lives there; `land` does those for a ship. When the task taught you something, `stow` says where it goes.
